   $q$A classmate suggests replacing sentence 8 with "Many students probably liked science more after the bus came." Why is the original sentence 8 better?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Sentence 8 is a direct quotation from Moana, and quotations are always the best evidence.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misidentifies-quotation"},{"text":"Sentence 8 gives a specific, measured result from the article, while the suggestion is a guess with no evidence.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The suggested sentence is much too long and would make the paragraph hard to read.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"length-not-relevance"},{"text":"The suggested sentence is about a different county than the one in the article.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Sentence 8 gives a specific, measured result from the article, while the suggestion is a guess with no evidence.$q$, $q$"Twice as many" students signing up is a measured fact from the article, while "probably liked" is the writer's guess. Sentence 8 is a paraphrase, not a direct quotation, so that reason is wrong.$q$, 'draft', 'f10969c8-3da3-54c7-a142-f56f2f62264c')
